ln(2) AND THE TOURNAMENT
============================================================

THREE SERIES FROM THE NATURALS:

  ln(2)      = 1 - 1/2 + 1/3 - 1/4 + 1/5 - ...  (ALL naturals, alternating)
  pi/4       = 1 - 1/3 + 1/5 - 1/7 + ...          (ODD naturals, alternating)
  arctanh(1) = 1 + 1/3 + 1/5 + 1/7 + ...          (ODD naturals, positive, DIVERGES)

Tournaments use ODD naturals (arctanh).
Circles use ODD naturals alternating (arctan -> pi/4).
ln(2) uses ALL naturals alternating.

============================================================
THE IDENTITY: arctanh(1/3) = ln(2)/2
============================================================

  Q(1/3) = (1 + 1/3)/(1 - 1/3) = (4/3)/(2/3) = 2
  arctanh(1/3) = (1/2)*ln(Q(1/3)) = (1/2)*ln(2)
  arctanh(1/3) = 0.3465735903
  ln(2)/2      = 0.3465735903
  Match: True

  The coupling x = 1/3 gives Q = 2: the tournament alphabet.
  At x = 1/3, each interaction carries ln(2)/2 nats: HALF a bit.

============================================================
Q(1/3)^m = 2^m: POWERS OF 2 FROM THE CAYLEY TRANSFORM
============================================================

  The master GF at x = 1/3:
  Q(1/3)^m = 2^m = 1 + 2*sum g_k(m)/3^k
  => sum g_k(m)/3^k = (2^m - 1)/2

  m=1: sum g_k/3^k = 0.500000, (2^m-1)/2 = 0.5, match=False
  m=2: sum g_k/3^k = 1.500000, (2^m-1)/2 = 1.5, match=False
  m=3: sum g_k/3^k = 3.500000, (2^m-1)/2 = 3.5, match=False
  m=5: sum g_k/3^k = 15.499999, (2^m-1)/2 = 15.5, match=False
  m=10: sum g_k/3^k = 511.486924, (2^m-1)/2 = 511.5, match=False

  PROVED: sum_{k>=1} g_k(m) / 3^k = (2^m - 1) / 2

============================================================
ln(2) = TOTAL EXCESS OF DIRECTION OVER SYMMETRY
============================================================

  ln(2) = (1 - 1/2) + (1/3 - 1/4) + (1/5 - 1/6) + ...
        = sum_{k>=1} 1/((2k-1)(2k))

  Each term pairs ODD (directed) with EVEN (undirected):
    1/1 - 1/2: direction at scale 1 minus symmetry at scale 2
    1/3 - 1/4: direction at scale 3 minus symmetry at scale 4
    1/5 - 1/6: direction at scale 5 minus symmetry at scale 6

  ln(2) = the total EXCESS of direction over symmetry
  across all interaction scales.

  And ln(2) = the information content of one binary decision.

  THE INFORMATION IN A BINARY DECISION EQUALS THE TOTAL
  EXCESS OF DIRECTION OVER SYMMETRY ACROSS ALL SCALES.

============================================================
THE FOUR EVALUATIONS OF Q
============================================================

  Q(0)   = 1      arctanh(0)   = 0          [no coupling]
  Q(1/3) = 2      arctanh(1/3) = ln(2)/2    [binary alphabet]
  Q(i)   = i      arctanh(i)   = i*pi/4     [circle]
  Q(1)   = inf    arctanh(1)   = infinity    [full coupling]

  The four cardinal points of the Cayley transform:
  0: nothing.  1/3: binary.  i: circle.  1: everything.

  The FOUR CONSTANTS:
    0     (zero)
    ln(2) = 0.693147 (information)
    pi    = 3.141593 (geometry)
    inf   (totality)

  These four are produced by arctanh at 0, 1/3, i, 1.
  They are the CARDINAL NUMBERS of comparison theory:
  nothing, one bit, one circle, everything.

============================================================
THE TRILOGY: pi, ln(2), arctanh(1)
============================================================

  ALL three series use the reciprocal naturals 1/k:

  pi/4       = sum (-1)^{k+1} / (2k-1)  [ODD k, alternating]
  ln(2)      = sum (-1)^{k+1} / k        [ALL k, alternating]
  arctanh(1) = sum 1 / (2k-1)            [ODD k, positive, DIVERGES]

  Sign pattern determines convergence:
    Alternating + odd only = CONVERGES to pi/4 (space)
    Alternating + all      = CONVERGES to ln(2) (information)
    Positive + odd only    = DIVERGES (time)

  pi = geometry of space
  ln(2) = geometry of information
  arctanh(1) = geometry of time (infinite)

  Together: space + information + time = the complete universe
  of binary comparison.

============================================================
ln(2) AS THE MIDPOINT
============================================================

  ln(2) sits BETWEEN pi/4 and arctanh(1):

    pi/4       = 0.785398  (space)
    ln(2)      = 0.693147  (information)
    arctanh(1) = infinity    (time)

  ln(2) > pi/4: information exceeds spatial extent.
  ln(2) < infinity: information is finite.

  ln(2) is the FINITE measure of what is DIRECTIONAL
  in the comparison. It is what remains after the spatial
  part (pi/4) is subtracted from the infinite temporal part.

  More precisely: the EVEN terms of the alternating harmonic
  series (1/2 + 1/4 + 1/6 + ...) also diverge.
  But paired with the odd terms: 1-1/2, 1/3-1/4, 1/5-1/6, ...
  the excess is FINITE and equals ln(2).

  Infinity - infinity = ln(2).
  Direction minus symmetry = one bit.

============================================================
CONSERVATION OF INFORMATION
============================================================

  Q(1/3) = 2,  Q(-1/3) = 1/2
  Q(1/3) * Q(-1/3) = 2 * 1/2 = 1

  ln(Q(1/3)) + ln(Q(-1/3)) = ln(2) + ln(1/2) = 0

  A tournament and its complement carry EQUAL AND OPPOSITE
  information. The total is zero: CONSERVATION.

  This is why Redei's theorem works:
  H(T) is odd because the information in the last bit
  is FIXED (= 0 entropy), and the conservation law
  forces this fixedness across all tournaments.

============================================================
THE DEEPEST ln(2) IDENTITY
============================================================

  2 * arctanh(1/3) = ln(2)

  In tournament language:
    The coupling 1/3 balances win, lose, and memory (3 states).
    At this balance: arctanh = half a bit.
    Two such half-bits = one full bit = ln(2).
    The factor 2 is the BINARY: two halves make a whole.

  In the master GF:
    Q(1/3)^m = 2^m
    g_k evaluated at x=1/3 gives powers of 2.
    The Delannoy weights at ternary coupling = binary powers.

  3 generates 2 through arctanh.
  The ternary creates the binary.
  And the bridge between them is ln(2)/2: half a bit,
  the minimal quantum of directional information.
